{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgrso7sP8ghXYBLANydbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01X2JcwTQUyfoprUprX8TZiL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"mkdir -p .nightralph && cat > .nightralph/findings.md <<'EOF'\n- [ ] Client-only Preact render leaves a real component's host empty; README/ADR-010 promise it renders itself, and the test hides this with a hand-written setup\n    src/preact.browser.ts:43, README.md:1571, docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md:68. Core's `connectedCallback` (src/factory.ts:133) never renders into an empty host (AGENTS.md says this too). `render(h(toPreact(El), { count: 2 }), div)` for an ordinary `define(...).withProps(...).render(...).setup(...)` component gives `<x-el></x-el>` with no children, and refs that setup reads (collected before setup) are null. The browser test `client-only render` (test/preact-browser.ts:19-37, 92) passes only because its fixture copies its render output into `host.innerHTML` inside setup, which no real component (e.g. `number-input`) does. Either the adapter or core must render the inner HTML on a client-only mount, or the docs must drop the claim and say what the component has to do. The test should use a component that does not render itself in setup.\n- [ ] Declared props are never assigned to the element during `hydrate`, so prop-only (and any non-attribute) values passed through the adapter are lost\n    src/preact.browser.ts:40-43. Preact 10.29.7 (node_modules/preact/src/diff/index.js:531-536) skips every non-function prop while hydrating, then stores those vnode props as `oldProps`. With `items: p.prop([])` and `hydrate(h(PC, { items: list }), root)`, the server never serialized `items` (prop-only), the attribute path cannot carry it, and Preact never sets `el.items`. Later renders that pass the same `list` reference also skip it (`oldProps[i] === value`), so the element keeps `[]` permanently. ADR-010 and the README say declared props reach the host as properties, and nothing mentions the hydrate exception. No test covers a declared prop whose value is not reflected in server attributes (the re-render test at test/preact-browser.ts:65 changes `count`, which hides this). A fix could assign the declared props in a ref or layout effect after hydrate.\n- [ ] `renderHost` output is no longer byte-identical when an extra attr has the same name as a declared prop\n    src/render-support.ts:67 (`attrs[k] ??= v`). Before this change, `El.render({ n: 1 }, { attrs: { n: '2' } })` emitted `<x n=\"1\" n=\"2\">`. Now it emits `<x n=\"1\">`. The spec says that rewriting `renderHost` on top of parts must keep its output byte-identical. The new behavior is probably better, but it is an unrecorded behavior change: it is not in CHANGELOG or in ADR-009/010.\nEOF\ncat .nightralph/findings.md | head -3","description":"Write review findings file"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":943,"cache_read_input_tokens":79822,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":943},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5315d5cc-ff1b-47b5-b928-3130c18aca4f","uuid":"fabc247a-0c31-4f45-84b4-7a2c68c787b9","timestamp":"2026-11-09T06:25:24.037Z","request_id":"req_011Cgrso7fEGMVCez6652fGX","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01X2JcwTQUyfoprUprX8TZiL":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/microtags.review && mkdir -p .nightralph && cat > .nightralph/findings.md <<'EOF'\n- [ ] Client-only Preact render leaves a real component's host empty; README/ADR-010 promise it renders itself, and the test hides this with a hand-written setup\n    src/preact.browser.ts:43, README.md:1571, docs/adr/ADR-010-preact-adapter-ownership-split.md:68. Core's `connectedCallback` (src/factory.ts:133) never renders into an empty host (AGENTS.md says this too). `render(h(toPreact(El), { count: 2 }), div)` for an ordinary `define(...).withProps(...).render(...).setup(...)` component gives `<x-el></x-el>` with no children, and refs that setup reads (collected before setup) are null. The browser test `client-only render` (test/preact-browser.ts:19-37, 92) passes only because its fixture copies its render output into `host.innerHTML` inside setup, which no real component (e.g. `number-input`) does. Either the adapter or core must render the inner HTML on a client-only mount, or the docs must drop the claim and say what the component has to do. The test should use a component that does not render itself in setup.\n- [ ] Declared props are never assigned to the element during `hydrate`, so prop-only (and any non-attribute) values passed through the adapter are lost\n    src/preact.browser.ts:40-43. Preact 10.29.7 (node_modules/preact/src/diff/index.js:531-536) skips every non-function prop while hydrating, then stores those vnode props as `oldProps`. With `items: p.prop([])` and `hydrate(h(PC, { items: list }), root)`, the server never serialized `items` (prop-only), the attribute path cannot carry it, and Preact never sets `el.items`. Later renders that pass the same `list` reference also skip it (`oldProps[i] === value`), so the element keeps `[]` permanently. ADR-010 and the README say declared props reach the host as properties, and nothing mentions the hydrate exception. No test covers a declared prop whose value is not reflected in server attributes (the re-render test at test/preact-browser.ts:65 changes `count`, which hides this). A fix could assign the declared props in a ref or layout effect after hydrate.\n- [ ] `renderHost` output is no longer byte-identical when an extra attr has the same name as a declared prop\n    src/render-support.ts:67 (`attrs[k] ??= v`). Before this change, `El.render({ n: 1 }, { attrs: { n: '2' } })` emitted `<x n=\"1\" n=\"2\">`. Now it emits `<x n=\"1\">`. The spec says that rewriting `renderHost` on top of parts must keep its output byte-identical. The new behavior is probably better, but it is an unrecorded behavior change: it is not in CHANGELOG or in ADR-009/010.\nEOF\ncat .nightralph/findings.md | head -3","description":"Write review findings file"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01X2JcwTQUyfoprUprX8TZiL":{"cwd":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.review"}}}
